# Base .gitignore for the Skomi SDK and plugin repositories.
#
# ⚠ GIT DOES NOT READ THIS FILE WHERE IT SITS. A .gitignore is only read from
# inside a repository's working tree, and this folder is not a repository — the
# ten below each are. So this is a BASE to copy into each of them:
#
#   for d in */; do cp .gitignore "$d"; done
#
# Copying rather than sharing is also the right answer for public repositories:
# the rules only reach a contributor who clones if they are committed in the
# repository itself. (`git config core.excludesFile` would work on this machine
# and nowhere else.)
#
# Derived from the monorepo's .gitignore with everything monorepo-shaped removed
# — the `sdk/<name>/` path prefixes, the four ASP.NET hosts, the module drop
# folder, the recordings folder, the generated stylesheets, the vendored skills.
# What is left is the union of the ten ecosystems, and only rules that are safe
# in ALL of them.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FOUR THINGS THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT IGNORE
#
# Each one is a file some template would swallow and every one of them is source
# in at least one of these repositories.
#
#   *.csproj      skomi-sdk-dotnet ships Skomi.Sdk.csproj, and skomi-sdk-unity
#                 ships Skomi.Sample.Core.csproj. The usual Unity template
#                 ignores *.csproj because a Unity PROJECT generates them; a
#                 Unity PACKAGE does not, and these are hand-written.
#
#   *.meta        Unity writes one beside every file and it carries the asset's
#                 GUID. For a package consumed from git they MUST be committed,
#                 or every clone regenerates them with new GUIDs and every
#                 reference into the package breaks.
#
#   Samples~/     Unity's convention: the trailing tilde is what stops the
#                 editor importing a sample until somebody asks for it. See the
#                 negation beside `*~` below — a plain backup-file rule matches
#                 the whole directory and `git status` says nothing at all.
#
#   *.lock files  package-lock.json and the sample's pubspec.lock are committed:
#                 a sample somebody copies should install what it was verified
#                 against. Only the Flutter PACKAGE's own lock is ignored, and
#                 it is anchored to the repository root so it cannot reach the
#                 sample beside it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --------------------------------------------------------------- the machine
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini

# --------------------------------------------------------------- the editors
.vs/
.idea/
*.iml
*.suo
*.user
*.userosscache
*.sln.docstates

# Editor backup files.
#
# ⚠ The negation is not optional. `*~` matches the DIRECTORY `Samples~`, and git
# does not descend into an excluded directory — so without this the whole Unity
# sample is invisible to `git status` and gets left out of the commit silently.
# Un-ignoring the directory is what lets the files inside it be seen again.
*~
!Samples~/

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ .NET
[Bb]in/
[Oo]bj/
*.nupkg
*.snupkg

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Node
node_modules/
dist/
.build-test/
*.tsbuildinfo
npm-debug.log*

# ------------------------------------------------- Gradle, Android, Kotlin
.gradle/
build/
local.properties
captures/
.kotlin/

# ⚠ NOT gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar. It is a binary, it looks like build
# output, and it is the one file without which `./gradlew` cannot bootstrap on a
# fresh clone.

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Flutter, Dart
.dart_tool/
.packages
.flutter-plugins
.flutter-plugins-dependencies

# The PACKAGE's lock file only — anchored to the repository root, so the sample
# application's own lock beside it stays tracked. A library pins nothing for its
# consumers; an application pins everything for itself.
/pubspec.lock

# The sample's generated platform folders. `flutter create .` writes them, and a
# committed set pins the sample to one Flutter version.
sample_todo/android/
sample_todo/ios/
sample_todo/linux/
sample_todo/macos/
sample_todo/windows/
sample_todo/web/

# -------------------------------------------------------- Swift, SwiftPM, Xcode
.build/
.swiftpm/
DerivedData/
Package.resolved
*.xcodeproj/
*.xcworkspace/
xcuserdata/

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Unity
#
# A Unity PACKAGE repository has none of these itself — they appear the moment
# somebody opens a consuming project in the same tree, which is exactly when
# they would otherwise be committed by accident.
#
# See the header: *.meta and *.csproj are NOT here, on purpose.
[Ll]ibrary/
[Tt]emp/
[Ll]ogs/
[Uu]serSettings/
[Mm]emoryCaptures/
*.unitypackage
*.apk
*.aab

# -------------------------------------------------------- PHP, WordPress
vendor/

# ⚠ The plugin zip is BUILD OUTPUT and belongs on a release, never in the tree.
# A committed one is a second copy of the plugin that goes stale the first time
# somebody edits the PHP — and a stale zip installs perfectly well, which is why
# nothing would tell you.
*.zip

# --------------------------------------------------------------- everything
*.log
*.tmp
